Root cause:
The bug arises from the potential overflow of the `mmap_xa_page` variable when inserting new mmap entries into the xarray. Since `mmap_xa_page` is limited to 32 bits, adding a large enough value (derived from the length of the memory being mapped) could exceed this limit, leading to incorrect behavior or crashes. The original code did not check for this overflow, which could result in invalid memory access or corruption of the xarray structure.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ucontext->mmap_xa_page += DIV_ROUND_UP(length, PAGE_SIZE);
```
Reason:
This statement increments `mmap_xa_page` without checking if the addition would cause an overflow, which can happen if the resulting value exceeds the 32-bit limit, leading to incorrect behavior or memory corruption.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (check_add_overflow(ucontext->mmap_xa_page,
                       (u32)(length >> PAGE_SHIFT),
                       &next_mmap_page))
    goto err_unlock;
ucontext->mmap_xa_page = next_mmap_page;
```
Reason:
These statements introduce a check for overflow before updating `mmap_xa_page`. If an overflow is detected, the function safely exits without making any changes, thus preventing potential memory access issues or corruption of the xarray structure.


Now, determine whether the provided code snippet is buggy:
{
	struct efa_mmap_entry *entry;
	int err;

	entry = kmalloc(sizeof(*entry), GFP_KERNEL);
	if (!entry)
		return EFA_MMAP_INVALID;

	entry->obj = obj;
	entry->address = address;
	entry->length = length;
	entry->mmap_flag = mmap_flag;

	xa_lock(&ucontext->mmap_xa);
	entry->mmap_page = ucontext->mmap_xa_page;
	ucontext->mmap_xa_page += DIV_ROUND_UP(length, PAGE_SIZE);
	err = __xa_insert(&ucontext->mmap_xa, entry->mmap_page, entry,
			  GFP_KERNEL);
	xa_unlock(&ucontext->mmap_xa);
	if (err){
		kfree(entry);
		return EFA_MMAP_INVALID;
	}

	ibdev_dbg(
		&dev->ibdev,
		"mmap: obj[0x%p] addr[%#llx], len[%#llx], key[%#llx] inserted\n",
		entry->obj, entry->address, entry->length, get_mmap_key(entry));

	return get_mmap_key(entry);
}

